          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"category\":\"intermittent_digitizer_channel_pair_readout_fault\",\"cause\":\"unknown\",\"action\":\"If raw data confirm intermittent loss or corruption of channels 32/33, inspect their shared digitizer/input path and channel mapping, compare channel presence before and after the subrun transitions, check digitizer synchronization and board status, and reseat the associated connections with the run stopped. Restart or reinitialize the affected digitizer only if the fault localizes to that hardware; then begin a new run and verify stable channel presence.\",\"confidence\":0.68,\"supporting_historical_runs\":[1620,2068,2126],\"reasoning_summary\":\"OBSERVED: anomalies occur in 740/741 subruns and are dominated by channel 32 across baseline, pulse-count, pulse-height, and occupancy features; channels 32 and 33 are also repeatedly reported missing. Both are trigger-masked. LVDS telemetry shows no persistently missing, zero, low, or high pin, including no stated persistent fault for their mapped pin. INFERRED: the paired missing-channel pattern and channel-32 waveform/statistics shift more strongly suggest an intermittent digitizer/readout-path or data-availability problem than persistent LVDS input failure; masking may explain trigger behavior but does not by itself establish why waveform channels are missing. Historical runs 1620, 2068, and 2126 provide only partial analogies involving digitizer lockup or missing channels; there is no exact match, and current LVDS evidence contradicts a straightforward LVDS-noise diagnosis. UNKNOWN: raw channel-presence timing, whether 32/33 share the same failing board/input, board matching, synchronization, queue occupancy, digitizer rates, and within-run configuration changes.\"}"
        }
